program            = 004_format_specifier_conformance
area               = 11_literals_and_strings
description        = Width-normalized printf conversions, flags, field widths and precisions producing identical output on every target
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour by construction:
- Every conversion specification exactly matches the type of its argument after the
  default argument promotions. Integer arguments are int, unsigned int, long long or
  unsigned long long, with the matching conversion; the short and signed char
  arguments are promoted to int by the variadic call and converted back by their
  length modifiers, which is exactly what those modifiers are defined to do; the float
  argument is cast to double explicitly, making the promotion visible rather than
  implicit.
- No signed integer overflow. Every value is a literal or an initialized object whose
  value is representable in its declared type: short holds -300, signed char holds
  -100, long long holds -9007199254740993, unsigned long long holds the 64-bit
  maximum, unsigned int holds 4000000000 and 4294967295.
- No shift operations of any kind, so no shift count can be out of range.
- No array indexing and no pointer arithmetic. The only pointer values passed are
  string literal addresses consumed by the string conversion, and no pointer value is
  printed.
- No string literal is modified; every literal is read only.
- No aliasing violation and no uninitialized read: every object is initialized at its
  declaration, and the volatile objects have static initializers.
- No object is modified twice between sequence points; nothing depends on padding bytes
  or on the addresses of unrelated objects.
- NOTHING DEPENDS ON UNSPECIFIED EVALUATION ORDER, enforced structurally rather than
  argued. An access to a volatile object IS an observable side effect, so the four
  volatile objects are each read exactly once, in a statement of its own, and only the
  resulting snapshots are passed to the conversions. No argument list in this program
  contains more than one volatile access. This replaced two earlier forms that each
  depended on unspecified order: one call read two different volatile objects in a
  single argument list, and another read the same volatile object twice in one argument
  list. The snapshots do not weaken the runtime coverage, because they are themselves
  loads from volatile storage, so every conversion is still fed a value the optimizer
  may not fold.
- Floating-point values are printed at fixed precision with margin, using only finite
  values, with no reliance on any default precision, so no rounding difference can be
  observed. No not-a-number and no infinity is produced or assumed.
- Verified empirically: the default strict warning gate (-Wall -Wextra -pedantic
  -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror) produces zero diagnostics, which
  includes the format-versus-argument checking that -Wall enables, and
  -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all runs to completion with no
  output and exit status 0.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Every format specification in this program is width-normalized, and this is what makes
the output identical on all four targets. The measured target differences are handled
by construction, never by a marker:

1. sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86_64, aarch64 and
   riscv64. No long, no size_t and no pointer is printed, and no long length modifier,
   size_t length modifier or pointer conversion is used anywhere. The only sizeof
   results printed are sizeof(int) and sizeof(long long), which are 4 and 8 on all
   four targets, and each is cast to int before printing.
2. long double has three different representations across the four targets (measured
   16, 12, 16 and 16 bytes, x87 80-bit versus IEEE binary128). No long double value is
   produced and no long double length modifier is used. That type is covered instead
   by 13_floating_point/004_long_double_target_restricted, which is the only program
   permitted to restrict a comparison on its account.
3. Plain char signedness is implementation-defined (signed on x86_64 and i686,
   unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64). The only char datum printed is the literal 'Q',
   whose value 81 is positive on every target.
4. unsigned int is 32-bit on all four targets, so the unsigned, octal and hexadecimal
   lines, including the all-ones runtime values, are invariant. short is 2 bytes and
   signed char is explicitly signed on all four targets, so the short and signed char
   lines are invariant.
5. All four targets are little-endian, and in any case no byte-order-sensitive value
   is printed.

Escape handling, hexadecimal and octal formatting and character-literal values were
measured byte-identical across all four targets at every optimization level, and the
floating-point results were measured byte-identical across all twelve target and
optimization configurations, so this program is fully cross-comparable and all three
oracles are enabled.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
